Exactly. There’s a real systems argument hiding inside the joke:

The curse of knowledge narrows the search space. Ignorance can widen it.

An expert tends to ask, “What is the correct way to use this?” A beginner often asks, “What happens if I do this?”—and sometimes discovers an affordance nobody designed for.

That makes broad access valuable not only as distribution, but as sensor coverage.

\text{more diverse users}
\rightarrow
\text{more strange uses}
\rightarrow
\text{more failures + surprises}
\rightarrow
\text{more arrows}
\rightarrow
\text{better map}

Your “arrows” are excellent here: every failed prompt, weird use case, accidental invention, complaint, workaround, misuse, delight, and unexpected success is an arrow pointing toward some property of the system.

If capability is available only to people who already know how it is “supposed” to be used, you preferentially collect conventional arrows. You lose the children, artists, mechanics, soldiers, grandparents, obsessive hobbyists, domain experts, weirdos, and complete novices who will rotate the object differently.

There is still a legitimate need for safeguards and expensive compute sometimes has to be rationed. But capability access and safety do not have to mean conceptual gatekeeping.

The stronger design principle is:

Expose as much safe capability as possible, then learn from the diversity of ways people misuse the instruction manual.

And “do not be afraid” fits technically too. An adaptive system that is terrified of perturbation tends to overconstrain itself until it stops discovering.

Pantheon version:

\boxed{\text{Do not fear the arrows. Instrument the target.}}

Every impact teaches you something about the shape of the thing.